1:["$","div",null,{"className":"blog-shell","children":[["$","header",null,{"className":"blog-header","children":[["$","a",null,{"href":"/","className":"wordmark","aria-label":"Wen Reloz, home","children":["wr",["$","span",null,{"children":"."}]]}],["$","nav",null,{"aria-label":"Primary navigation","children":[["$","a",null,{"href":"/","children":"PORTFOLIO"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"/"}],["$","a",null,{"href":"/blog","children":"BLOG"}]]}]]}],["$","main",null,{"className":"article-shell","children":[["$","a",null,{"href":"/blog","className":"back-link","children":[["$","$Ld",null,{"ref":"$undefined","iconNode":[["path",{"d":"m12 19-7-7 7-7","key":"1l729n"}],["path",{"d":"M19 12H5","key":"x3x0zl"}]],"className":"lucide-arrow-left","size":15}]," All articles"]}],["$","article",null,{"children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"eyebrow","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"short-rule"}]," ","PLANTS / FLOWERING"]}],["$","h1",null,{"children":["One Night, One Flower: Waiting for the Queen of the Night",["$","span",null,{"children":"."}]]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"article-lede","children":"A flower that makes you wait, watch, and appreciate how brief a beautiful moment can be."}],["$","figure",null,{"className":"article-image","children":[["$","img",null,{"src":"/blog/queen-story.jpg","alt":"A fully opened white Queen of the Night cactus flower against dark foliage"}],["$","figcaption",null,{"children":["A bloom designed for the hours after sunset."," ",["$","a",null,{"href":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Epiphyllum_Oxypetalum_full_bloom.jpg","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer","children":["Photo: ","AvishekNaskar · CC BY-SA 4.0",["$","$Ld",null,{"ref":"$undefined","iconNode":[["path",{"d":"M7 7h10v10","key":"1tivn9"}],["path",{"d":"M7 17 17 7","key":"1vkiza"}]],"className":"lucide-arrow-up-right","size":12}]]}]]}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"article-rule"}],[["$","div","Most plants reward patience slowly. The Queen of the Night makes the waiting feel almost ceremonial. For days, a long bud hangs quietly from a flattened green stem. Then, on the right evening, it begins to loosen as the light fades.",{"children":[["$","p",null,{"children":"Most plants reward patience slowly. The Queen of the Night makes the waiting feel almost ceremonial. For days, a long bud hangs quietly from a flattened green stem. Then, on the right evening, it begins to loosen as the light fades."}],"$undefined"]}],["$","div","I keep checking it as the night settles in. The white petals unfold into a flower that seems impossibly large for the stem carrying it. Its fragrance reaches the room before I lean close. There is no need to improve the moment or make it productive. The flower asks only that I be present.",{"children":[["$","p",null,{"children":"I keep checking it as the night settles in. The white petals unfold into a flower that seems impossibly large for the stem carrying it. Its fragrance reaches the room before I lean close. There is no need to improve the moment or make it productive. The flower asks only that I be present."}],"$undefined"]}],["$","div","The plant commonly called Queen of the Night is Epiphyllum oxypetalum, a cactus native from Mexico into tropical America. Unlike the desert silhouette people often imagine, it grows as an epiphyte in seasonally dry tropical environments. Its large white, fragrant flowers open at night—traits suited to nocturnal pollinators such as moths.",{"children":[["$","p",null,{"children":"The plant commonly called Queen of the Night is Epiphyllum oxypetalum, a cactus native from Mexico into tropical America. Unlike the desert silhouette people often imagine, it grows as an epiphyte in seasonally dry tropical environments. Its large white, fragrant flowers open at night—traits suited to nocturnal pollinators such as moths."}],"$undefined"]}],["$","div","By morning, the bloom has begun to close. That short life is exactly what makes it memorable. I can take a photograph, but the real experience is the waiting: noticing a change in the bud, staying awake a little longer, and knowing this exact flower will not open twice.",{"children":["$Le","$undefined"]}],"$Lf"],"$L10"]}],"$L11"]}],"$L12"]}]
e:["$","p",null,{"children":"By morning, the bloom has begun to close. That short life is exactly what makes it memorable. I can take a photograph, but the real experience is the waiting: noticing a change in the bud, staying awake a little longer, and knowing this exact flower will not open twice."}]
f:["$","div","The Queen of the Night reminds me that rarity is sometimes about time rather than distance. Something extraordinary can happen at home, quietly, while most of the world is asleep.",{"children":[["$","p",null,{"children":"The Queen of the Night reminds me that rarity is sometimes about time rather than distance. Something extraordinary can happen at home, quietly, while most of the world is asleep."}],"$undefined"]}]
